--TEST--
One batch refused at its second run leaves its first run in place on a `Core\Db\Connection` and takes it back out on a `Core\Db\Transaction`, so § 4's "a batch is not a transaction" is a statement about the receiver — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, `rule:core-classes/db-transactions` and `rule:classes/no-traits`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Transaction::executeMany
use Core\Db;
use Core\Db\DbError;
use Core\Db\Transaction;

// One statement run three times, refused at its second run by the unique
// index. Both receivers are asked the identical question, because what differs
// is the handle and not the batch: `rule:classes/no-traits`'s delegation gives them one body,
// and § 7 is the only thing that acts between the runs.
var $db = Db::connect("main");
$db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key, name text not null unique)", []);
$db->execute("insert into t (name) values (?)", ["ada"]);

array<array<mixed>> $rows = [["bo"], ["ada"], ["chen"]];

// On the connection each run stands on its own, so the row the first run wrote
// is still there after the second is refused.
try {
    $db->executeMany("insert into t (name) values (?)", $rows);
    echo "the connection ran the whole batch\n";
} catch (DbError $refused) {
    echo "connection stopped\n";
}
echo "after the connection ", $db->query("select id from t", [])->count(), "\n";

// Back to one row, so the two halves are asked the same question.
$db->execute("delete from t where name = ?", ["bo"]);

// Through a transaction the refusal travels out of the closure, and the row the
// first run wrote goes back out with it. Counting rather than reading a line is
// what makes this fail for a member that committed each run of the batch.
try {
    $db->transaction(fn (Transaction $tx): uint =>
        $tx->executeMany("insert into t (name) values (?)", $rows));
    echo "the transaction ran the whole batch\n";
} catch (DbError $refused) {
    echo "transaction stopped\n";
}
echo "after the transaction ", $db->query("select id from t", [])->count(), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, and
# `:memory:` gives this case a database nothing else has written to.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
connection stopped
after the connection 2
transaction stopped
after the transaction 1
